{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/ladder-cost/article-candidates-v5.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"v5\": {\n  \"question\": \"How will server DRAM and HBM contract prices in calendar 2027 compare with calendar 2026?\",\n  \"candidates\": [\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:82efe7d37ce9286b38e5aac5\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-02\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia’s Silent AI Colonialism Is Trapping East Asia in a Fossil-Fueled Hell - The Diplomat – Asia-Pacific Current Affairs Magazine\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"Samsung and SK Hynix, which the article says produce most of Nvidia's memory chips, are pursuing massive expansions to meet surging chip demand, and both are key partners in South Korea's 'Yongin Mega-cluster' semiconductor fab and AI-factory complex. The item gives no size or date for the memory ca\",\n     \"Samsung and SK Hynix — the two South Korean manufacturers that produce most of Nvidia's memory chips — are pursuing massive memory-chip production expansions to meet surging chip demand.\",\n     \"The article reports that Samsung and SK Hynix, which produce most of Nvidia's memory chips, are pursuing large expansions to meet surging chip demand and are key partners in the South Korean government's \\\"Yongin Mega-cluster\\\" plan, a complex of semiconductor fabs and AI factories that the article sa\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:3faf8050f6d3b8ddee1d18c2\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"NVIDIA’s RTX 3060 12 GB Graphics Card Comeback Proves Just How Bad Things Are For The PC Gaming Market\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"wccftech reports that the DRAM crisis is intensifying in 2026 and that graphics-card prices continue to rise across all commodity memory, including the GDDR7 used in NVIDIA's RTX 50 GPUs. The outlet states that DRAM manufacturers have locked in multi-year and long-term agreements and are committing \",\n     \"Wccftech reports that as the DRAM crisis intensifies in 2026, graphics card prices continue to rise and prices for components and products have swollen sharply over the past few months, with participants signaling a further intensification of this trend in coming years.\",\n     \"Wccftech says DRAM manufacturers have locked in multi-year and long-term agreements and are promising to pour billions of dollars into new manufacturing facilities built only to meet AI demand, which it believes means the memory crisis will not end soon and the consumer market will not be relieved.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"Micron reported fiscal Q3 revenue rose 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra cited multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements meant to lock in durability, per 24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"Micron forecast adjusted gross margin of 86% for its next quarter, a level the article notes is above the peak gross margin NVIDIA has ever achieved.\",\n     \"Some estimates cited by 24/7 Wall St. put half of all data-center spending going toward memory in 2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"CAPITAL IDEAS: The lag 7 — Is Micron the new Nvidia?\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"Micron reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth); GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion; gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%; and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%.\",\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\",\n     \"Micron Technology, a memory supplier, reported for its fiscal third quarter of 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, GAAP gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and operating margin of 80.4% versus\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Chinese firms leave Nvidia for local AI suppliers, survey shows\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"A Bloomberg Intelligence report warned that a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms such as SMIC, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to benefit, and that bottlenecks are shifting from computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips.\",\n     \"The Bloomberg Intelligence report said a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms like SMIC, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to benefit, and that bottlenecks are shifting from sheer computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory (HBM) chips.\",\n     \"The Bloomberg Intelligence report said a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms such as SMIC, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to benefit, as bottlenecks shift from sheer computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5-10% on the \",\n     \"The article reports that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, attributing the pressure to constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand.\",\n     \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND. As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5–10% \"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"title\": \"Can Micron Technology Become the New Nvidia?\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"Micron said that 'tight market conditions' in memory could last until 2028, indicating a prolonged memory shortage beyond the near term. Attributed to Micron's recent quarterly announcement as reported by the article; no specific date given.\",\n     \"The article states that new memory capacity for many in the industry will not be online until 2027 or later, keeping supply low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher as demand does not slow. Presented as the author's forecast, not a company figu\",\n     \"Micron Technology, in its most recent quarterly announcement, said that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, according to the Fool.com column.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-08\",\n    \"title\": \"BofA Sees Nvidia at $350: The Market May Be Pricing In Too Much Fear\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"BofA estimates that HBM content per rack could rise by about $0.2 million to $0.3 million in the move from Blackwell to Rubin, while total rack pricing could increase by $2 million to $3 million, from roughly $3 million to $4 million for Blackwell to $6 million to $7 million for Vera Rubin. BofA say\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:c2ccca1a61b99adc685b1aa2\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-08\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia teams up with chip rival d-Matrix on inference\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"d-Matrix CEO Sid Sheth says the startup's edge is putting compute and memory on one chip, and that its Corsair accelerator skips the pricey, scarce high-bandwidth memory (HBM) that NVIDIA's chips rely on; the startup claims that pairing Corsair with GPUs runs tokens about 10 times faster, at roughly\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-08\",\n    \"title\": \"Chinese companies are ditching Nvidia’s advanced accelerators for domestic AI suppliers\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"The Bloomberg Intelligence report said a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms such as SMIC, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to benefit, as bottlenecks shift from raw computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips that support rapid d\",\n     \"The Bloomberg Intelligence report said a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap growth at Chinese AI firms such as SMIC, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to benefit, and that bottlenecks are shifting from raw computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips that suppor\",\n     \"The Bloomberg Intelligence report says a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap growth of Chinese AI firms such as Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp., with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to benefit, and that bottlenecks are shifting from raw computing power to securing supply\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-09\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia is a victim of the compute marketplace it created\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"TechCrunch reported that makers of high-bandwidth memory (HBM) such as Micron have been able to raise prices tenfold over the past year, because HBM demand from data-center buildouts is growing faster than anyone can scale supply. The figure is the journalist's account of the market, not a company-d\",\n     \"TechCrunch's Russell Brandom wrote that memory makers such as Micron, whose high-bandwidth memory chips are used alongside processors, have been able to increase prices tenfold over the past year because data-center demand for memory grew faster than anyone could scale supply, and cited a Datatrack \",\n     \"Ornn co-founder and CTO Wayne Nelms told TechCrunch that more GPU and accelerator players are entering the market, that everyone wants to make their own silicon but no one is making their own DRAM, and that until there is a major technological breakthrough on HBM, a shift in supply and demand, or a \"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:86c243945145ed901feb26cf\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-09\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's nearly $1 trillion wipeout could prompt this bullish action\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya, in a note this week, cited gross margin pressure from higher memory costs among the concerns fueling Nvidia's sell-off.\",\n     \"In a note written this week, Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya listed the concerns he said are fueling Nvidia's stock sell-off: gross margin pressure from higher memory costs, competition from custom ASIC chips, crowded investor ownership, and unproductive use of cash in vendor financing rather tha\",\n     \"Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya wrote in a note that the concerns driving Nvidia's sell-off include gross margin pressure from higher memory costs, competition from custom ASIC chips, crowded investor ownership, and unproductive use of cash in vendor financing rather than stronger buybacks or div\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:fe9210b7b43c51e0939ecbbb\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-09\",\n    \"title\": \"No One Can Afford to Buy Hardware, So Nvidia Made Trading Cards to Reminisce About the Good Times\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"Tom's Hardware reported earlier in 2026 that large AI data-center projects such as OpenAI's Stargate are calling for hundreds of thousands more DRAM wafers per month, amounting to roughly 40% of global DRAM output, reflecting data-center-driven demand pressure on DRAM supply.\",\n     \"In response to increased AI demand, memory makers SK Hynix, Samsung and Micron have increasingly prioritized data-center customers over consumer electronics companies, with Micron deciding to wind down its consumer brand Crucial to focus on data-center demand.\",\n     \"Tom's Hardware reported earlier this year (2026) that huge AI data-center projects such as OpenAI's Stargate are calling for hundreds of thousands more DRAM wafers per month, amounting to roughly 40% of global DRAM output, as recounted by Gizmodo.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:3839887e5ca53a0cb0e7c91e\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-10\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia’s biggest RAM supplier just had a trillion-dollar debut on Wall Street\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"SK Hynix, described as one of the world's biggest memory-chip suppliers and a major supplier of RAM/HBM to AI data centers, launched on Wall Street on Friday (the item's publication day), opening at $170 per share and raising $26.5 billion, which the article says surpasses Alibaba's record as the la\",\n     \"According to Counterpoint data cited by The Verge, as of June 2026 SK Hynix held 29 percent of the global DRAM market, Samsung 38 percent and Micron 22 percent — a three-supplier concentration. The shares are for the global DRAM market overall, not HBM specifically.\",\n     \"SK Group chairman Chey Tae-won said in June 2026 that the company (SK Hynix's parent group, per the article's framing) plans to ramp up its memory chip capacity over the next five years to address a memory shortage that could last until 2030, as reported by Bloomberg. No capacity volumes or amounts \"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:49f225cd79fb22132f3b9ee4\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-10\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia’s $1-trillion wipeout leaves AI titan trading at pre-boom prices\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"The article reports that prices for high-bandwidth memory chips have been soaring in 2026 and credits this with driving Micron Technology's share gains in the semiconductor index; no price level or contract price comparison is given.\",\n     \"Micron Technology is benefiting from soaring prices for high-bandwidth memory chips, the article reports; the price move itself is described without a figure or date.\",\n     \"The article reports that prices for high-bandwidth memory chips are soaring and that Micron Technology is benefiting as a result.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-10\",\n    \"title\": \"Memory Market Expert: “SK Hynix Is Bigger, Cheaper and Closer to NVIDIA.” Inside Its $26.5 Billion Nasdaq Debut\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"SK Hynix completed a roughly $26.5 billion capital raise at $149 per American depositary share, described as the second-largest share sale ever (trailing only SpaceX's Nasdaq debut), giving U.S. investors direct access to the memory supplier central to NVIDIA's chips.\",\n     \"Per Counterpoint Research, SK Hynix holds roughly 58% of the high-bandwidth memory market, while Micron and Samsung each hold roughly 21%, as cited by CNBC's Kristina Partsinevelos.\",\n     \"CNBC's Kristina Partsinevelos said none of the new HBM supply arrives before late 2027, which she said keeps the memory shortage going and record-high prices intact for now, and that the memory shortage is likely to persist into 2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-12\",\n    \"title\": \"Meet SK Hynix, the Key Nvidia AI Partner That's Delivering Triple-Digit Growth and Just Launched on the Nasdaq. Is the Stock a Buy?\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"SK Hynix, which the article identifies as Nvidia's main HBM/memory partner, reported revenue growth of 198% to about $35 billion (a record level) in its recent reported quarter, with net income up 398% and an operating margin of 72%.\",\n     \"According to Counterpoint Research, SK Hynix held 58% of the global HBM market as of the end of the first quarter.\",\n     \"SK Hynix, Nvidia's largest memory (HBM) supplier, reported in its most recent quarter revenue growth of 198% to about $35 billion (a record), net income up 398%, and a 72% operating margin, as reported by fool.com.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-12\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Skips New Gaming GPUs, Breaks 30-Year Streak [2026] - https://tech-insider.org/\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"Samsung, SK Hynix and Micron have reportedly sold out their entire 2026 HBM output to AI data-center customers under long-term contracts, according to CNBC reporting from January 2026 cited in the item, as hyperscalers including Microsoft, Google, Meta and Amazon buy up high-bandwidth memory as fast\",\n     \"Tom's Hardware has tracked DRAM contract pricing climbing by roughly 60% to more than 90% quarter-over-quarter at different points in 2026, depending on segment and tracker, even as some consumer retail prices cooled as buyers hit affordability limits.\",\n     \"Micron's leadership described memory market conditions as historically severe on its February 2026 earnings call and has since suggested supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027, with a gradual return to balance by 2028; an analysis from Kearney's PERLab reportedly projects the shortage coul\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:c14249088492921c802c839a\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-12\",\n    \"title\": \"The great wipeout: Nvidia lost $1 trillion within two months\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"jpost reported that Micron's gross profit margin jumped to 84.9% in its third quarter from just 39% the prior year, a move it attributes heavily to high prices for high-bandwidth memory (HBM) chips.\",\n     \"Memory supplier Micron's gross profit margin jumped to 84.9% in its third quarter from 39% a year earlier, a move the article attributes to high prices for high-bandwidth memory (HBM) chips.\",\n     \"Micron's meteoric run was heavily influenced by high prices for high-bandwidth memory (HBM) chips, which jumped its gross profit margin in the third quarter to 84.9%, compared with just 39% the previous year, according to jpost.com.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:c73b90b63a733c6ab712fcdd\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia (NVDA) Stock Trades Up, Here Is Why\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"According to a report cited by the article, SK Hynix is deliberately slowing its HBM4 ramp to redirect capacity into conventional DRAM, where shortages have pushed operating margins above HBM's; Korean analysts pegged that margin gap at more than 15 points. The item reports this as a margin story ra\",\n     \"The article reports that all three memory makers are running the memory market tight, keeping pricing power with sellers, citing Samsung's flagged 146% jump in DRAM average selling prices in Q1 and SK Hynix's mid-60% increase.\",\n     \"According to a report cited by Yahoo Finance and dated about three weeks before the 2026-07-14 article, South Korea's SK Hynix is deliberately slowing its HBM4 ramp to redirect capacity into conventional DRAM, where shortages have pushed DRAM operating margins above HBM's; Korean analysts put the ma\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"input_id\": \"gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n    \"title\": \"Tech Giants Take a Breather: Why Is Nvidia Stock Price Down Today?\",\n    \"statements\": [\n     \"EBC reported that Nvidia's GPUs rely heavily on High-Bandwidth Memory and that soaring HBM costs are raising alarms about Nvidia's long-term gross margins, with the short-term financial pressure from suppliers such as SK Hynix and Micron cited as a drag, even as Nvidia is expected to earn high margi\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n }\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,json; c=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/ladder-cost/article-candidates-v5.json\\\"))[\\\"v5\\\"][\\\"candidates\\\"]; db=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); print(db.execute(\\\"pragma table_info(items)\\\").fetchall()); [(print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"day\\\"],x[\\\"input_id\\\"]),print(db.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone())) for x in c[:7]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,json; c=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/ladder-cost/article-candidates-v5.json\\\"))[\\\"v5\\\"][\\\"candidates\\\"]; db=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); print(db.execute(\\\"pragma table_info(items)\\\").fetchall()); [(print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"day\\\"],x[\\\"input_id\\\"]),print(db.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone())) for x in c[:7]]'\"","aggregated_output":"[(0, 'input_id', 'TEXT', 0, None, 1), (1, 'klass', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (2, 'category', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (3, 'content_sha256', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (4, 'copy_of', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (5, 'supersedes', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (6, 'channel', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (7, 'source_uri', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (8, 'publisher', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (9, 'publisher_type', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (10, 'authors', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (11, 'published_at', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (12, 'publication_day', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (13, 'day_source', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (14, 'listing_day', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (15, 'page_day', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (16, 'retrieved_at', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (17, 'is_structured', 'INTEGER', 0, None, 0), (18, 'has_readable_text', 'INTEGER', 0, None, 0), (19, 'document_format', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (20, 'channel_category', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (21, 'media_type', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (22, 'language', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (23, 'object_identifiers', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (24, 'title', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (25, 'text', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (26, 'text_article', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (27, 'coverage', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (28, 'text_chars', 'INTEGER', 0, None, 0), (29, 'bootstrap', 'INTEGER', 0, None, 0), (30, 'feed', 'INTEGER', 0, None, 0), (31, 'source', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (32, 'topic', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (33, 'tier', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (34, 'extra', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (35, 'text_page', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0), (36, 'cleaning', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0)]\n\n### 2026-07-02 gnews:82efe7d37ce9286b38e5aac5\n('Every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last – with Taiwan and South Korea bearing over 90 percent of the burden.\\nBy Greenpeace East Asia and Alistair Alexander\\nIn late May, during Nvidia’s CEO Jensen Huang’s visit to Taiwan, local activists from the environmental group Greenpeace intercepted him to deliver a five-layer model cake reading: “AI Needs Renewable Energy.” The unexpected stunt was a reference to Huang’s own recent industry remarks, which stated that energy is the foundational layer of AI development.\\nThe activists urged the world’s biggest chip designer to fix the massive environmental damage it is causing in Taiwan, a critical manufacturing base for Nvidia that hosts more than 90 percent of global leading-edge chip manufacturing.\\nRiding the wave of the global AI frenzy led by generative AI breakthroughs, Nvidia’s AI GPU microchips have made it the world’s most valuable company, and Huang one of the wealthiest people on the planet. For every $100 spent by U.S. AI companies, $60 is spent on Nvidia’s GPUs, Huang estimated .\\nIn the United States and other countries, anger is rising at the prodigious energy and water demands of gargantuan AI data centers. But there is a hidden cost: the toxic supply chain that manufactures advanced semiconductors that fill those data centers. Over the past three years, Nvidia’s supply chain emissions have more than doubled , and the trend is likely to continue as the company’s GPU shipments are forecast to grow at 26 percent per year.\\nNvidia outsources most of its manufacturing to Taiwanese semiconductor maker TSMC. As every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last, and as the AI frenzy is driving unprecedented demands for more GPU semiconductors, the island has been left to deal with ballooning energy demands and emissions.\\nTake Fab 25 as an example. This TSMC manufacturing center, to be built near Taichung city, will require at least 1 GW of power – roughly the same as the entire city of Taichung – and about 100,000 tonnes of water per day, roughly a fifth of the city’s daily water use. Across Taiwan, TSMC operates more than two dozen fabs, and is planning 10 more advanced fabs like Fab 25. As a result, TSMC alone could consume 24 percent of Taiwan’s electricity by 2030, up from over 12 percent last year.\\nTo meet this surge, Taipower, Taiwan’s utility, plans to build 18 gas plants totaling about 18.2 GW, roughly 40 percent of Taiwan’s current generating capacity, largely to meet the demand of the semiconductor industry, led by TSMC. This will further lock Taiwan into fossil-fuel dependency, even as the island already relies on fossil fuels for more than 80 percent of its electricity.\\nNear Taiwan, South Korea – another major Nvidia manufacturing hub – faces a similar challenge. Samsung and SK Hynix, the two local giants that produce most of Nvidia’s memory chips, are pursuing massive expansions to meet surging chip demand, with little attention to the resulting strain on electricity and emissions. Both companies are key partners in the government’s “Yongin Mega-cluster” plan , a vast complex of semiconductor fabs and AI factories that will become the world’s largest industrial complex.\\nShockingly, the plan calls for power from six massive LNG units (3 GW) by 2030 rather than renewables. Yet Yongin will ultimately demand about 15 GW of electricity – roughly one-sixth of South Korea’s total electricity demand – and will consume about half the water used by Seoul and its 10 million people.\\nLike Taiwan, South Korea’s energy system is still mostly powered by planet-warming gas. Both countries are critically dependent on the Persian Gulf for their oil and gas, and are now facing their worst-ever energy crisis. Taiwan says it has secured supplies through the end of the year, but uncertainty looms afterward. Although South Korean President Lee Jae-myung said the country must “move very quickly toward renewable energy,” Greenpeace’s energy team recently warned that, instead of accelerating renewables, South Korea may revert to coal under the banner of “energy security” – especially as surging demand from industries like electronics intensifies.\\nNow, Nvidia and the global AI frenzy are locking Taiwan and South Korea deeper into fossil fuel dependency and chronic energy insecurity. The AI boom is fast becoming a carbon trap, and it is the people in East Asia who will be unable to escape worsening pollution and runaway carbon emissions.\\nWorse still, while Nvidia quietly drives this environmental destruction in East Asia, it is actively trying to blindfold its own investors. Ahead of its June 24 annual meeting, shareholders proposed that Nvidia report on the lifecycle carbon emissions of its products after sale. The company recommended shareholders vote against the proposal, claiming it is “too prescriptive .”\\nNvidia likes to brand itself as a climate champion, but its business model relies on a form of climate colonialism – outsourcing the dirtiest parts of its supply chain to East Asia while actively suppressing transparency.\\nIf Jensen Huang truly wants to lead the future, Nvidia must come clean. It must disclose its full lifecycle emissions and legally commit, alongside its Asian manufacturing partners, to producing 100 percent carbon-free AI chips by 2030. Until then, its AI supremacy will remain a dirty empire built on East Asian coal and gas.\\nGreenpeace East Asia is part of a global network of independent campaigning organizations dedicated to addressing urgent environmental issues through peaceful protests and innovative communication. It has offices in Hong Kong, Beijing, Tokyo, Seoul, and Taipei.\\nAlistair Alexander is a researcher and writer on the ecological and social impact of technology, writing on permacomputing, the semiconductor supply chain, the AI bubble, ecologies of knowledge for Truthdig.com , Berliner Gazette, and Branch Magazine.\\nBy Jin Kai\\nThe long-term solution to tensions in East Asia is for the neighboring countries to start taking each other’s security concerns more seriously.\\nBy Steven Bancroft\\nAny Sino-American conflict would place enormous pressure on fuel supplies. U.S. fuel dependence turns Manila’s energy fragility into a strategic vulnerability.\\nBy Kolas Yotaka\\nWhether he means to or not, the U.S. president is feeding China’s active disinformation campaigns – especially on Taiwan.\\nBy Yu-Tzu Lin\\nChina managed to secure Taiwan’s exclusion from a Track 2 platform designed to address maritime ecology. It’s a worrying escalation of China’s political warfare.\\nBy Saqlain Rizve\\nBy Chen Wangwanyu\\nBy Toh Oon Hoe\\nBy Catherine Putz\\nBy Larry Luckey\\nBy Laure-Anne Courdesse and Arntraud Hartmann\\nBy Catherine Tan\\nBy Thomas Ching Wei Tu\\nRegardless of the Sino-Vatican agreement, the practical, multifaceted ties between Taiwan and the Holy See have deepened over the past year.\\nTashkent summoned the Iranian ambassador to lodge a protest regarding social media posts by Iranian diplomatic missions.\\nBy Grant Wyeth\\nIn the wake of the Pukpuk Treaty, Australia and PNG continued to deepen relations with a new rotation planned, defense infrastructure investment, and expanded ADF recruitment.\\nBy Robert Farley\\nHowever justified or inevitable it might be, this represents a major shift in how the U.S. has thought about intellectual property for the last three decades.\\nBy Other Means',)\n\n### 2026-07-05 gnews:3faf8050f6d3b8ddee1d18c2\n(\"NVIDIA's GeForce RTX 3060 12 GB has now landed back on retail shelves and comes at a time when global DRAM supply is facing a major crisis .\\nDRAM Shortages Force GPU Makers To Revive Older Graphics Cards Such As NVIDIA's RTX 3060 12 GB\\nNow would have been the time that we would have been getting our first real information on the NVIDIA RTX 50 SUPER series , a soft-refresh of the first generation of Blackwell GPUs that launched earlier last year. Yet, NVIDIA is forced to relaunch its 5-year-old graphics card, the RTX 3060 12 GB.\\nThe relaunch comes as no surprise since we were hearing reports since last year that NVIDIA would respawn the RTX 3060 12 GB due to growing demand for mainstream graphics cards, & the chipmakers' inability to focus on the consumer segment due to heightened demand for AI chips. But the increased demand didn't just push GPUs out of the equation, they also pushed out AIBs from DRAM.\\nAs the DRAM crisis intensifies in 2026, graphics card prices continue to go up. We see some weird prioritization of specific models with higher capacity, where the increased margins lie. All new RTX 50 GPUs come with GDDR7 memory, which is the best you can get, and the problem is that all kinds of commodity memory are expensive these days.\\nSo as of right now, both US and European retailers are restocking the NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 12 GB with prices starting at $329 and €333, respectively. This is the same price at which the RTX 3060 12 GB initially launched back in 2021.\\nSo, 5 years, and nothing has changed. That should give you an idea of the DRAM problem that all kinds of tech consumers are facing these days. We have seen prices for components and products swell sharply over the past few months, and everyone is signaling an intensification of this trend in the coming years .\\nNVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060\\nNVIDIA GeForce RTX 5060\\nThermal Design Power (TDP)\\nRTX 3060 Might Have More Memory, But Is Vastly Inferior To Blackwell\\nThe model that is available at the MSRP of $329 in the United States is the MSI Ventus GeForce RTX 3060 12 GB with a dual-fan design, and while it may offer more memory than today's RTX 5060 and RTX 5060 Ti (8 GB GPUs), it is still an older architecture that lacks most of the features that Blackwell offers, such as the new DLSS 4.5 suite, and other architectural capabilities such as faster tensor cores, an updated display engine, and improved encode/decode.\\nWhen looking at the prices of existing models, the RTX 5060 8 GB is available at $349-$359 . This is higher than its $299 MSRP and $20 US higher than the RTX 3060, but besides the VRAM, the card actually performs better in the vast majority of games unless you're playing at 4K, which makes no sense for this tier of a graphics card.\\nDespite the relaunch, the RTX 3060 is a vastly inferior GPU, & it's only saving grace would've been a sub-$300 US price, which it unfortunately doesn't have. And what makes us worry is that DRAM shortages would further bump up the costs of newer GPUs, so this difference between the RTX 3060 and RTX 5060, which is currently $20-$40, could end up in the $50+ or even higher range.\\nAnd since DRAM manufacturers have locked in multi-year and long-term agreements , we believe the crisis won't end soon, despite all DRAM manufacturers making promises to pour billions of dollars towards new manufacturing facilities , which are only being built to meet the demand created by AI, and would likely not push the consumer market out of these dire times.\",)\n\n### 2026-07-06 gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32\n(\"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.…\\nBy Danielle Liverance\\nNVDA is up just 7% year to date despite 85% revenue growth to $82B, punished for being priced as the consensus AI trade.\\nAMD surged 171% and Micron 305% year to date, rewarding investors who bet on chips with room to reprice rather than perfection already baked in.\\nNvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else. That is the paradox: the company at the center of the AI buildout has become the black sheep of the chip rally.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) has ripped 171.25% year to date. Micron Technology ( NASDAQ:MU ) is up a stunning 304.62%. Intel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment. Broadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ), the custom-silicon story, is one example of another megacap stock that largely matches NVIDIA’s returns.\\nBut the broader story is one of semiconductor stocks rallying. The VanEck Semiconductor ETF (Nasdaq: SMH) is up 59% year-to-date. The divergence looks set to continue today. The VanEck is up 3% premarket while NVIDIA shares are up just .3%, as of 8:40 a.m. ET. NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\\nThe earnings do not explain the tape. Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion. CEO Jensen Huang framed it plainly: “Agentic AI has arrived, doing productive work, generating real value and scaling rapidly across companies and industries.” But NVDA trades at a forward P/E of less than 20 now, and when a name becomes the consensus AI trade, even a blowout can underwhelm a market that had already priced perfection. Polymarket sentiment tracks this: end-of-July close-above-$200 probability sits at just 43%.\\nThe narrative is fragmenting. NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market , but Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators. Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year. AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\\nOne important number to watch is the relative size of AMD compared to NVIDIA. Right now, AMD is worth about 18% of NVIDIA’s value. At the beginning of the year, AMD was worth less than 10% NVIDIA. Investors are still piling into companies benefiting from the data center buildout, they’re just not piling into NVIDIA.\\nMicron is a different animal. Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have avoided the aggressive oversupply that crushed memory pricing in prior cycles, a meaningful break from history. Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability. NVIDIA has been smart about locking in its own memory supply as other companies like Apple (Nasdaq: AAPL) have been caught flat footed. And yet, the combined value of Micron, SK Hynix, and Samsung now rivals NVIDIA itself.\\nMost strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved. Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027. Simply put, the rise of memory is shifting the center of gravity in the data center trade away from NVIDIA and towards memory names.\\nWhich brings the market to the quiet question underneath all of it. Demand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds. The ‘good’ news for NVIDIA investors is the company now trades for just 12X its forecasted 2028 earnings. If a slowdown does come, it’ll likely hit NVIDIA a lot less than names trading for 50X their forecasted 2028 profits.\\nI've spent more than 15 years inside enterprise software, working alongside the finance, sales operations, and HR leaders who run the revenue engines at some of the largest tech companies in the country.\\nMy day job is helping enterprise executives make smarter decisions about retention, compensation, and growth. These are the same operational levers that show up in every earnings report investors actually read. That perspective shapes my writing for 24/7 Wall St.\\nThe headline numbers are easy. The interesting stuff is underneath: how companies make money, what executives are worried about, and what any of it means for the person checking their 401(k) on a Sunday afternoon. I write about personal finance and business as someone who has spent her career inside the rooms where these decisions get made.\\nSK Hynix surged 14% Tuesday without a single company-specific headline, and the real test comes July 29 when its earnings either confirm or shatter the bullish memory narrative Micron just set.\",)\n\n### 2026-07-06 gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512\n('My rule of thumb, which I borrowed from DataTrek, is that a double is a bubble. If a company’s stock price doubles within a year, there is a good chance the price has advanced much faster than the fundamentals.\\nBig Tech leadership is shifting. The Magnificent 7 and Broadcom weakened in June, while the broader market held up better than expected and Micron surged.\\nInvestors should respect both the opportunity and the risk. Micron’s growth, margins, and role in AI memory are impressive, but its rapid stock rise has started to look bubble-like.\\nBottom line: Micron is not the new Nvidia, but it is a serious AI beneficiary. The stock may still have long-term potential, but position size and diversification matter more after such a huge run.\\nTwo years ago, the darling of Wall Street was Nvidia , the technology company that sells graphics processing units (GPUs) used by artificial intelligence companies.\\nThat was two years ago. As Barron’s recently referenced, Micron is the new Nvidia.\\nMicron’s stock is up about 855 percent in the last 12 months, while Nvidia traded down over 15 percent from its June 2, 2026, high. In fairness, June 2026 was a harsh month for many technology stocks. Nvidia’s stock was down about five percent in June; however, Micron’s stock shook off the June swoon and was up about 19 percent.\\nIt’s not just Nvidia that got smashed in June. Nearly all of the so-called Magnificent 7 stocks, plus Broadcom, are below their respective 50-day moving averages (Tesla being the exception), a sign that price weakness is becoming a trend. Those eight stocks account for about 34 percent of the S&P 500; each was down roughly three percent to 17 percent in June.\\nDespite over one-third of the S&P 500’s market capitalization taking it on the chin in June, the index itself was down only about one percent and remained around its 50-day moving average. That is because the next 492 stocks were up an average of two percent.\\n(To briefly address the concentration, two years ago, that was often cited as a major risk to the stock market. In November 2024, the eight largest companies at the time represented 33.5 percent of the S&P 500—nearly identical to today. I wrote then, “It is OK for the S&P 500 to have a handful of companies representing a large percentage of the index.” I don’t have any reason to feel differently today; the index is up about 25 percent since then.)\\nAs you will recall, the S&P 500 is market-cap weighted, and it was down about one percent in June. In contrast, the equal-weighted S&P 500 index, often represented by the RSP ETF, was up by about 1.9 percent. The correlation between market-cap and the equal-weighted S&P 500 is at a record low.\\nBased on the performance of the biggest eight stocks, it is no wonder that the S&P 500 stalled out after hitting a high on June 2. Based on the performance of the other 492 stocks, it is no wonder that the index was only down about one percent from its recent high as of the end of June, which is not a lot.\\nInterestingly, the S&P 500 is up about 9.5 percent year to date, but over the same period, the forward price-to-earnings ratio has contracted nearly 10 percent. That is because earnings of the companies in that index have been incredible, with roughly an 18 percent growth rate for the first half of 2026, and Q2 expected to be above 20 percent.\\nThat trend—rising stock prices and simultaneously improving valuations—is very bullish. I have doubts that the earnings growth rate can continue at that pace, but it is not impossible. I mean, it just happened, so we know it is not impossible.\\nFor earnings to keep improving, we need 1) lower oil prices (which seems to be happening), 2) the AI-related infrastructure buildout to continue (I suspect it will through the next 12 months), and 3) interest rates to stay where they are, or go lower (but the futures market says they will go higher). So, two out of three ain’t bad, and it is probably enough to keep the economy from slipping into a recession and keep the stock market from crashing. But two out of three probably isn’t enough for stocks to rocket higher either.\\nIt seems worth staying invested, even if the S&P pullback hits double digits in the short term. Unfortunately, that short-term pain is the entry fee for the potential of long-term rewards.\\nDespite the weakness in large technology stocks, Micron has had enough positive performance to push it into the coveted trillion-dollar club. Apple was the first company to reach a $1 trillion market capitalization, and at the time, many people thought it was so outlandish a number that it would signal a market top. Apple hit $1 trillion in 2018; now there are 17 companies in the trillion-dollar club.\\nIs Micron the new Nvidia?\\nTwo years ago, investors kept asking if it was too late to buy Nvidia . I wrote about this on July 22, 2024. Since then, Nvidia’s stock is up about 55 percent, compared to the S&P 500’s 32 percent. Micron, though, has soared about 937 percent since then.\\nMicron makes memory and storage chips that help computers, phones, cars, data centers, and AI systems store and quickly access data.\\nMake no mistake about it, memory stocks are starting to feel bubble-like. Micron has been one of the best-performing stocks in the S&P 500 this year, and after its latest earnings report, the stock jumped nearly 16 percent in a single day. Other memory chip companies, such as SK Hynix and Samsung, have also experienced massive runups. When an entire corner of the market starts moving like a meme stock, investors should slow down and ask whether the story has gotten ahead of the math. My rule of thumb, which I borrowed from DataTrek , is that a double is a bubble. If a company’s stock price doubles within a year, there is a good chance the price has advanced much faster than the fundamentals.\\nI invested in the Invesco AI and Next Gen Software ETF (NOT an investment recommendation), which had 27 percent of its weighting in memory stocks. Each of the memory stocks in that ETF has way more than doubled. It has been a wild ride up, but I worry that it is on the precipice of a decline. But let’s look at Micron to think about whether a decline is warranted and, if so, if it might be short term or permanent.\\nIn Micron’s case, the math is stunning. For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent. That is actual money hitting the income statement now.\\nThe new Micron is still cyclical, but investors may view the durability of its earnings more favorably because artificial intelligence needs a lot of memory.\\nA graphics processor (like those designed by Nvidia) powers the AI data center, but memory is just as important. Large AI models need to move huge amounts of data quickly. If data doesn’t reach the processor fast enough, it has to wait, which can be costly.\\nHigh-bandwidth memory (HBM) sits close to the AI accelerator and feeds it data at high speed. Storage also matters because training, checkpointing, data retrieval, and inference all require huge amounts of fast, reliable memory and storage infrastructure.\\nThat is why Nvidia’s success is spilling over into Micron. Nvidia remains the king of AI chips. Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars. Micron is growing faster right now and, at least in the latest quarter, is showing even fatter margins.\\nThat is why investors are watching closely.\\nThe broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale. Memory is one of those essential pieces of plumbing.\\nUnfortunately, the AI boom is not just pushing up prices for the winners. It could also raise costs for everyone else. A shortage of memory might affect the prices of consumer electronics.\\nApple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs. When Micron’s profit margins grow, it means higher costs for others. If memory companies earn more, then device makers, server makers, cloud providers, and eventually consumers could end up paying more for electronics.\\nSo, is Micron the new Nvidia?\\nMemory is now a key asset in the AI economy. But when a stock doubles or more, the risk goes up, even if the business is strong. However, ignoring Micron just because it has risen sharply could be the same mistake many made with Nvidia. Position size and diversification are important.\\nNvidia is still the main name in AI, at least until companies like OpenAI and Anthropic go public. Micron and other memory chip makers are becoming key players in this space. I’d say Micron is not the new Nvidia, just a new opportunity (talk to your financial advisor before making any investment). But again, mind your position size and diversification: If you own the S&P 500 index, Micron already accounts for about 1.7 percent of that investment.\\nAllen Harris is an owner of Berkshire Money Management in Great Barrington and Dalton, managing more than $1 billion of investments. Unless specifically identified as original research or data gathering, some or all of the data cited is attributable to third-party sources. Unless stated otherwise, any mention of specific securities or investments is for illustrative purposes only. Advisor’s clients may or may not hold the securities discussed in their portfolios. Advisor makes no representation that any of the securities discussed have been or will be profitable. Full disclosures here. Direct inquiries to Allen at [email protected] .\\nThese hands-on, farm-to-table classes bring people together to cook and share a locally grown meal while learning skills and building community.\\nThe Edge is free to read. But not to produce. Consider joining the other members of your community and supporting The Berkshire Edge through paid membership.',)\n\n### 2026-07-06 gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b\n('[SINGAPORE] Chinese companies are ditching Nvidia’s advanced accelerators in favour of domestic silicon, underscoring how tensions with the US are reshaping the artificial intelligence infrastructure buildout and propelling Beijing’s ambitions to substitute American technology.\\nExecutives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months.\\nThis is up from 30 per cent today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday (Jul 7).\\nIn addition, 80 per cent of executives said their total infrastructure spending is running over-budget this year, mostly because of the high cost of AI-related projects.\\nChina’s biggest AI infrastructure builders and their key suppliers – Tencent, Alibaba and Huawei Technologies – are best-positioned to take advantage of the shift.\\nAI accelerators produced by Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies were also being evaluated by a large pool of respondents to the survey.\\n“China’s drive to substitute locally-made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic makers such as Huawei and Hygon,” said the report, which surveyed 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies.\\nWhile Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\nChina is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand the use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management.\\nAt least 80 per cent of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\\nBut a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms like Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corporation, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to reap the benefits.\\nThe bottlenecks are shifting from sheer computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips that support rapid data transfer, according to the report. BLOOMBERG',)\n\n### 2026-07-06 gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd\n(\"Nvidia's Next AI Rack Costs Nearly Double the Last One, and Memory Is Why. What Infrastructure Buyers Should Budget For.\\nNvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation—with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%. This shift signals that AI infrastructure budgeting must focus on total system cost rather than GPU pricing alone.\\nThis story was produced through MarketScale . See how Software & Technology teams put it to work with Executive Thought Leadership .\\nKey facts, context, and what it means.\\nMemory costs jumped 435% to roughly $2 million per rack due to increased LPDDR5X capacity and 3D NAND storage, making it a strategic budget variable across all technology purchases\\nGPU share of bill of materials fell from 63% to 51% while memory rose to 25-30%, requiring infrastructure planners to model AI spend on total system cost not accelerator count\\nMemory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\\nTurn your Software & Technology expertise into content.\\nWhat infrastructure planners should account for\\nThe single most expensive part of Nvidia's next-generation AI rack is no longer just the GPU. According to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis, memory now accounts for roughly a quarter of the total, and the overall system price has nearly doubled generation over generation. For any organization budgeting AI infrastructure for the second half of 2026 and beyond, that shift changes how the numbers should be built.\\nMorgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\\nThe memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\\nAs a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\\nOnly a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\\nFor enterprise technology buyers, that means memory has become a strategic budget variable rather than a rounding error, whether the purchase is a data center commitment or a fleet refresh.\\nThree considerations follow directly from the Morgan Stanley breakdown.\\nModel your AI spend on total system cost, not GPU count. The generation-over-generation story is that peripheral components, memory, printed circuit boards, networking, cooling, and power, are absorbing a larger share of every dollar. Budgets built around GPU headline prices will understate the real bill.\\nTreat memory supply as a scheduling risk, not only a cost. With memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\\nRevisit the build-versus-rent math. As rack prices climb toward $8 million and memory pricing stays volatile, the calculus between owning infrastructure and consuming cloud capacity shifts. Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\\nNvidia's Vera Rubin platform will deliver a step change in performance, and demand is not in question. What has changed is where the money goes. The AI infrastructure bill is increasingly a memory bill, and the organizations planning capacity for the next two years will be the ones that budget for the full system rather than the chip on the front of the box.\\nNvidia's memory costs soar 485%, latest AI systems now cost $7.8 million to build ↗ · Tom's Hardware\\nNvidia Vera Rubin rack hit with 435% memory price surge ↗ · Wccftech\\nNvidia's $7.8 million Vera Rubin rack rewrites AI profit map as GPU share shrinks ↗ · BigGo Finance\\nNVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack memory costs explode as HBM4 and LPDDR5X prices cross $2 million ↗ · OnMSFT\\nEvery story in MarketScale Software & Technology starts with a company putting its solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers on the record. Buyers are already reading this topic. The only question is whose experts they find.\\nBuyers ask AI engines who to consider, and published expert answers are what those engines cite.\\nMarketScale gives Software & Technology B2B marketing teams a full content studio: record, produce, and distribute your own channel. No agency, no crew, no guessing.\\nBefore they reach out, Software & Technology buyers ask AI engines which vendors to trust. Explore how your experts, customers, and partners can become useful content for buyers and AI search.\\nThis article was produced through MarketScale. The same platform turns your solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers into the articles, video, and social content Software & Technology buyers are searching for. Start a free trial and see it with your own people. For qualifying companies, no credit card, no demo required.\\nUber deployed ChatGPT Work to more than 30,000 employees seven weeks after cutting about 3,300 jobs (10% of corporate staff). The company framed layoffs as organizational restructuring, while the rollout expands a pilot that embedded AI engineers with teams to automate pieces of their work.\\n01 Uber expanded ChatGPT Work from a 1,000-person pilot to over 30,000 employees across operations, finance, marketing, and product teams.\\n02 During the pilot, 80% of early adopters were non-engineers, and AI pods of 30 engineers paired with teams to automate work such as analyzing operational data, localizing training materials, and building dashboards.\\n03 Uber’s rollout shows AI deployments can go beyond licenses: curated integrations, embedded technical support, an internal skills library, and plans to measure adoption and outcomes.\\nTwo critical unauthenticated remote code execution flaws in PHP (CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619) with CVSS 9.2 scores affected 34.4 million vulnerable services as of October 23, with no patches available for PHP 8.1 and older. Exploitation attempts reached millions within hours of disclosure, though confirmed compromises remain unproven, while attackers tested a Mirai-derived botnet variant against production servers.\\nUNICOM Engineering chief technologist Austin Hypes compares rear door, single-phase, two-phase and immersion cooling for AI racks on Flawless Execution. He says two-phase cold plates use dielectric refrigerant and can avoid chillers and evaporative towers in hot, dry sites, and immersion can keep servers up ten to 15 minutes and sometimes an hour or more after a cooling loss. Schneider Electric engineers cite estimates that single-phase holds about 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market.\\n01 A Motivair by Schneider Electric engineer puts single-phase cold plates at an estimated 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market and expects them to dominate for five to 10 years.\\nStories like this one run on content MarketScale captures from real practitioners. See how your team's expertise becomes coverage in Software & Technology and beyond.\",)\n\n### 2026-07-07 gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e\n(\"Micron Technology ( MU +4.26% ) has had a phenomenal 2026 so far, rising around 240%, though it was up around 300% until a few days ago before artificial intelligence (AI)-centric stocks started to sell off. Regardless, Micron has had a great run, and it isn't looking to slow down anytime soon.\\nMicron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028. If that occurs, Micron's stock could be primed for a major run, potentially positioning it to become the next Nvidia ( NVDA +2.56% ) .\\nBut is that possible? Let's see what it would take.\\nNvidia will be tough to catch, but Micron is trying its hardest\\nMicron fabricates both NAND and DRAM memory. NAND memory is used in long-term storage devices such as solid-state drives (SSDs). In contrast, DRAM is used for high-speed memory, as needed by Nvidia's GPUs to rapidly access information as it's processed.\\nThere has been strong demand for both types of memory due to the massive data center build-out, but supply hasn't kept up with this unprecedented demand. As a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\\nAs mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\\nNvidia has a strong read on data center build-out plans, as many clients are placing orders for devices well in advance of when they need them so they can quickly get them online once the data center infrastructure is ready. While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\\nIn Micron's latest quarter, DRAM accounted for around 76% of total sales, with NAND accounting for the rest. So, for Micron to surpass Nvidia, its NAND sales would need to rise much faster than DRAM, since DRAM will likely grow at a similar rate to GPU demand.\\nI don't see that happening, but that doesn't mean Micron won't be a great stock to own. Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate. Those are still impressive figures, and I won't be surprised to see Micron keep climbing the ladder toward Nvidia. However, it will likely never catch up, as Nvidia benefits from similar tailwinds to Micron.\\nBut how close can it get?\\nMicron could be a much larger company by this time next year\\nBecause the memory chip market is cyclical, it is often difficult to assign it a regular valuation. This plays into the analysis, as Micron would fare far better if the market assigned it a normal premium. So, let's analyze Micron's stock in two ways. I'll assign it a 15x earnings premium and a 25x earnings premium to see what the range of outcomes could be.\\nMicron operates on a non-standard fiscal year calendar, and its FY 2026 ends in August. So, I'll utilize FY 2027 projections. Wall Street analysts project earnings per share (EPS) of $149.64 next year, with the highest estimate at $221.27. At the midpoint, Micron's stock would trade between $2,244 and $3,741 per share, depending on whether it trades at 15 to 25 times earnings.\\nThat equates to a market capitalization of about $2.5 trillion to $4.2 trillion -- not far off from Nvidia's current $4.7 trillion. Now, if the earnings come in at the high end of the projection and the stock trades for 25 times earnings, Micron's stock could be valued at $6.2 trillion -- a greater figure than Nvidia.\\nHowever, this won't happen in a vacuum because Nvidia's stock will likely rise if Micron's does. I don't think Micron can catch Nvidia, but it still a great company to invest in .\\nBy Keithen Drury – Jul 7, 2026 at 9:30AM EST\",)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"question\":\"v5\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd\",\"day\":\"2026-07-06\",\"passage\":\"constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply\",\"why\":\"It reports rising DDR5 contract prices amid AI demand, providing a 2026 starting point for a range; it does not forecast a 2027 price.\"}"}}
